/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The noise at the receiver input is white.
   - Speech and music have most of their energy at low and middle frequencies; high notes and consonants are weaker.
   - Pre-emphasis fixes this before transmission.
   - The pre-emphasis network is a first-order boost set by a time constant τ = RC.
   - |Hpre(f)| = √(1 + (f/f1)²), f1 = 1/(2πτ)
   - f is the audio frequency in Hz; f1 the corner frequency in Hz; τ in seconds.
   - |Hde(f)| = 1/√(1 + (f/f1)²)
   - the same f1 at both ends, so the two responses multiply to 1 at every frequency.
   - A practical pre-emphasis network (R1 in parallel with C, then a shunt R2) has a second corner well above the audio band, so the boost stops rising there.
   - With de-emphasis, the output noise is the parabola f² weighted by 1/(1 + (f/f1)²).
*/
