/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Sending L and R on two carriers would double the spectrum, so the transmitter combines them into one baseband signal and frequency modulates the carrier with it.
   - Compatibility decides the layout.
   - The difference L − R carries the stereo information.
   - L = [(L + R) + (L − R)]/2, R = [(L + R) − (L − R)]/2
   - L and R are the left and right audio voltages; (L + R) comes from the 0–15 kHz band and (L − R) from the 38 kHz detector.
   - The difference channel is DSB-SC, which saves the power a 38 kHz carrier would take, but coherent detection needs a 38 kHz reference of the right phase.
   - The receiver filters out the pilot and doubles it with a phase-locked loop or a doubler: 2 × 19 = 38 kHz.
   - Phase matters.
   - separation = 20 log[(1 + cos φ)/(1 − cos φ)] dB
   - φ is the phase error of the recovered 38 kHz subcarrier. φ = 10° gives 42.3 dB; φ = 30° gives 22.9 dB.
*/
