/* book exam traps: The pilot is 19 kHz and the subcarrier 38 kHz. The 38 kHz carrier itself is suppressed and never sent. | The difference channel is DSB-SC, not SSB and not FM. It needs both sidebands, 23 to 53 kHz. | A mono receiver loses nothing: it plays L + R, the full programme; its 15 kHz low-pass removes the pilot and the difference band. | A phase error in the recovered 38 kHz scales L − R by cos φ; it does not mix L + R into the difference channel. | The pilot and L − R share the 75 kHz peak deviation; they do not add to it. In Carson's rule use the highest baseband frequency, 53 kHz, for stereo. */
